  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Severe thunderstorms possible again today with a \nfocus south and west of the I-95 metros. Damaging winds are the main \nthreat.\n\nMultiple clusters of showers and thunderstorms exist at mid\nmorning, ranging from stratified rain in the southern Shenandoah\nValley, to a decaying cluster in SW PA, to more potent storms in\nOhio. There is new development in northern IN, and another\ncluster in NW IL, both of which will be fed by a rich theta-e\nairmass and could potentially grow upscale. Locally, we are in a\nstable environment thanks to earlier decaying thunderstorms.\nAnvil level winds will likely keep sending debris cloudiness\ninto the area, and if low level winds remain west or\nnorthwesterly, it may be difficult to advect richer moisture\nback into the area. Therefore, the thunderstorm clusters to the\nwest may tend to either propagate southeastward along the\ninstability gradient or weaken with eastward extent. \nSouthwestern portions of the CWA may remain closest to this \nstorm track. Any MCSes which approach from the activity farther\nto the west likely wouldn't reach the area until later this\nevening or overnight. Thus their maintenance remains in\nquestion.\n\nA Flood Watch remains in effect through tonight for Garrett\nCounty due to the combination of ongoing flooding and expected\nadditional rainfall (possibly multiple rounds).\n\nPrevious discussion:\nHi-res guidance is all over the place when it comes to how\nupstream convection will evolve and impact our region in regards\nto both timing and coverage of storms based upon the convective\ndebris this morning. Some of the guidance has a linear feature\nworking across central WV into southwest VA perhaps grazing the\nsouthern Shenandoah Valley while other pieces of guidance have a\ncluster of storms working across the entirety of the forecast\narea between 19z-23z/3-7pm. Additionally a secondary batch of \nshowers and thunderstorms may come through during the overnight \nperiod into early Wednesday morning similar to what we are \nseeing now. SPC maintains a Slight Risk (Level 2 out of 5) for \neastern WV/far western MD and the Shenandoah Valley. A Marginal \nRisk (Level 1 out 5) has been outlooked elsewhere across the \nregion for through tonight. Damaging winds will once again be \nthe main threat along with isolated instances of flash flooding.\n\n\nBeyond today, timing, coverage, and locations of storms remains\nuncertain despite strong mid level flow and increased height \nfalls across the region. Shortwave disturbances will continue to\npulse through although more cloud cover may be noted which \ncould dampen the threat. Machine learning probabilities for \nsevere weather have decreased to around 5-20 percent with a \nfocus further south. These probabilities taper even more late \nweek as the front finally drops into the region. Once again \ndamaging winds will be the main threat with storms along with \nisolated instances of flash flooding. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers and thunderstorms remain possible at\ntimes Wednesday through Friday, with a cooling trend expected \ntoward the weekend.\n\nDaily storm chances look to continue through at least Friday as a \ncold front gradually drifts south. Ahead of that front will be \nseveral fast moving shortwave disturbances which will help aid \nin shower and thunderstorm development during the diurnal \nperiods. The southward progress of the cold front is uncertain\nhowever, and could be modulated by various convective complexes\nand a potential wave of low pressure tracking along it Thursday\nor Friday. Both physics-based and AI guidance show lower severe\nweather chances Wednesday through Friday with declining \ninstability. Showers and thunderstorms could remain possible \nthough with the front nearby. Thursday may be relatively the \nhottest day as heights briefly rise.\n\nFor Saturday in particular, there may be a better chance of \ndrying out as high pressure builds to the north. This will also \nbring a corresponding cool down in temperatures (potentially a \nbit below normal) along with lower humidity. The front won't be \ntoo far to the south however, and will likely attempt to lift \nnorth later this weekend or early next week as a low pressure \nsystem moves into the Great Lakes. Thus, some low end PoPs \nremain in the forecast each day through the end of the forecast \nperiod.",
